  "worked_solution": "If $g(4)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-4)$ is a factor of $g(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $g(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $g(x).$\n\nSince $(x-4)$ is a factor of $g(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $g(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $g(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+4}{x-4}={x}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-4)({x}^{2}-1)=(x-4)(x-1)(x+1).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial, arranged in ascending order, are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=1,\\text{ and}\\,x=4.\n$$",
